            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I'm hitting something similar with:\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set(\"x\", \"i1\", function()\n  -- no-op, empty range. other api functions might cause this too:\n  -- this is just the one that was failing for me\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, {})\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns, vim.pos.cursor(0):to_extmark())\nend)\n\nvim.keymap.set(\"x\", \"i2\", function()\n  if vim.api.nvim__mcursor_cascading() then\n    return\n  end\n  vim.print(\"this only prints once\")\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_text(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, {})\n  vim.api.nvim_buf_set_extmark(0, ns, vim.pos.cursor(0):to_extmark())\nend)\n```\n\nWhen there's no multicursors placed, these bindings work correctly. But when there's a multicursor placed, `i1` / `i2` is replayed at every cursor (including the new multicursor placed at the primary).\nSome interesting notes:\n- The _first_ multicursor that was placed in the buffer doesn't replay `lhs`, but all the others do.\n - Both keybinds work the same, even though the second one should prevent a cascade.",
